WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.490
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.770
Good day, good evening.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.180
Wherever you're joining us from.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.060
I'm Ann Kurth

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.880
and the Dean of the
Yale School of Nursing.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.100
And I'm really delighted
to welcome each of you

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.200
to the Global Health Conversation series

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.822
sponsored by the Yale Institute
for Global Health, YIGH.

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:27.430
The series is a venue for
our community to learn from

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.213
and connect with innovative
leaders in global health.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.640
In addition to YIGH,
we are indebted to the

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.190
George Herbert Walker, Jr.
Lecture in International Studies

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:38.990
at the Whitney and Betty MacMillan Center

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.560
for International and Area Studies at Yale

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.260
for co-sponsoring the series,
including today's event.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.220
I'm so pleased to be able to
welcome the over 700 people

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.870
who registered for today's
event, including faculty,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.720
students, and alumni of Yale University,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.220
colleagues at the UN Development Program

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.070
and at Permanent Missions
to the UN in New York

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.450
and the general public.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.603
It's a pleasure to be with you all today.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.440
As we are all seeing more
and more and experiencing

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.670
earth's life sustaining
ecosystems are being undermined,

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.070
threatening human health, other
species health, development

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.430
and survival.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.750
While we have seen increases
in the lifespan and declines

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.600
in child mortality and
notable reductions in poverty,

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.250
these advances are being
undermined by climate change,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.560
deforestation, loss of arable
land, freshwater depletion,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.403
ocean acidification and pollution.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.500
Today's conversation will
focus on the critical issue

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.600
of Planetary Health, which
stresses the urgent need

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.690
to better understand
relationships between these

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.970
global scale impacts
and population health,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.230
and to develop strategies
and policies to lessen

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.100
these impacts, repair natural systems

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.820
and maintain the planet in
a state that is conducive

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.773
to optimal population health.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.910
Planetary health has become
an increasingly important area

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.760
at Yale, with a variety of
initiatives across the campus.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.860
Through YIGH, we have recently launched

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.600
a planetary health network,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.690
which brings together
faculty members from across

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.650
the university who are passionate
about planetary health,

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.330
to collaborate on research projects

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.870
and knowledge sharing initiatives.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.470
Please look to the YIGH
website, if you're interested

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:17.773
in learning more or joining this network.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.840
In addition, the
university has launched the

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.320
Planetary Solutions Project
which draws on Yale's repository

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.570
of world-class knowledge,
innovation and leadership,

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.703
to address human caused
environmental change and crises.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.540
Building on this effort, we
are so pleased to have with us

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.830
today, Achim Steiner,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.520
Administrator of the
UN Development Program.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.140
For nearly three decades,
Administrator Steiner

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.720
has been a global leader
on sustainable development,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.940
climate resilience and
international cooperation.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.500
To formally introduce Administrator
Steiner, I'll now turn

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.363
to my colleague, Dr. LaRon Nelson.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.110
- Hello everyone.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.390
My name is LaRon Nelson
and I'm the Associate

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:00.928
in Global Affairs and Planetary Health

00:03:00.928 --> 00:03:02.810
at the Yale School of Nursing.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.790
Today I am pleased to be joined
by my fellow co-moderator,

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.810
Dr. Robert Dubrow,
Professor of Epidemiology

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.397
at the Yale School of Public Health

00:03:10.397 --> 00:03:12.380
and the Faculty Director
of the Yale Center

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.310
on Climate Change and Health.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.910
Thanks for being here today Rob.

00:03:17.147 --> 00:03:19.630
Rob Dubrow and I are
also the co-leads of the

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:22.677
Yale Institute for Global
Health Planetary Health Network.

00:03:22.677 --> 00:03:25.770
And we are excited that today's
event will spark new ideas

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.503
and interest on this
critically important issue.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.550
It is my pleasure to introduce
today's distinguished guest,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:33.800
Achim Steiner,

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.743
Administrator of the United
Nations Development Program.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.080
Over his entire career, he has
worked tirelessly to champion

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.810
sustainable development, green economy

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.930
and international cooperation.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.160
And has been a vocal
advocate of the 2030 Agenda

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.060
and the Sustainable Development Goals.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.840
Administrator Steiner has
served across the United Nation

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.540
system looking at global
challenges through the

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.060
sustainable development lens.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.480
Between 2006 and 2016, he led the

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.870
United Nations Environment
Program, the UN's leading

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.250
environmental authority
that sets the global

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.493
environmental agenda.

00:04:11.705 --> 00:04:14.940
In 2017 he was elected
by the United Nations

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.670
General Assembly to head the

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.800
United Nations Development
Program, which works with

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.390
170 countries to support
poverty eradication

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.853
and inclusive society.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.270
Achim Steiner had previously
held other notable positions

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.350
including Director of
Oxford Martin School,

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.600
Director General of
the International Union

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.220
for the Conservation of
Nature and Secretary General

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.063
of the World Commission on Dams.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.500
In December of last year,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.410
the United Nations Development Program

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.670
launched its Human
Development Report 2020.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.987
Which lays out a stark
choice for world leaders.

00:04:51.987 --> 00:04:55.400
"Take bold steps to reduce
the immense pressure

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.400
that is being exerted on the environment

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:01.637
and the natural world or
humanity's progress will stall."

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.160
The report argues that as
people and the planet enter

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.310
an entirely new geological
epoch, the Anthropocene

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.560
or the age of humans, it
is time for all countries

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.060
to redesign their path to progress,

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.690
while fully accounting for
the dangerous pressures,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.440
humans put on the planet and dismantled

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.860
the growth imbalances
of power and opportunity

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.213
that prevent change.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.860
During today's conversation,
I look forward to discussing

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.040
the reports and the important
issue of planetary health.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.540
Please join me in welcoming
Administrator Steiner.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:38.813
- Thank you.

00:05:38.813 --> 00:05:42.910
Thank you very much Dean
Kurt, Dean LaRon Nelson,

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.860
Professor Dubrow.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.950
I assume that this is where
I simply take off and jump in

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.040
unless somebody stops me.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.560
And let me begin by thanking you all for

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.160
this wonderful invitation, the
opportunity to spend an hour

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.740
and a half with you and
some of your faculty

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.648
and others who are
interested in this topic of

00:06:00.648 --> 00:06:02.150
"Planetary Health."

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.500
And I want to thank both the
Dean and President Nelson for

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.860
the very kind introduction
because what in a sense prompted

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.530
I think us to focus on this
issue of planets, people

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:18.070
and prosperity and planetary
health has really come

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:22.180
into focus, particularly
through our world of UNDP

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:26.360
through the Human Development
Report we published for 2020.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.770
And for those of you who
may not be so familiar,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.150
UNDP is the United Nations
Development Program.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.460
It's at the heart of the
UN development system.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.640
It's the entity that
works in 170 countries

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:41.330
on the major development
issues of our time.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.360
But it's also an
organization that obviously

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.590
with the experience it's
assembled from so many different

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.390
frontiers of development across the world,

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:52.610
tries to sometimes bring
into focus the challenges

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.260
and perhaps the horizons
against which we need to think

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.530
about the future of development.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.670
And so the topic of "Planetary
health" and the theme

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.630
of our 2020 Human Development
Report coincide very neatly.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.420
Because they both
recognize that we are at an

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:08.563
inflection point.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.500
And that inflection point
comes down to something very

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.650
common sense like, you
sometimes have to wonder why

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.820
it took us so long to
realize that human health

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.390
and environmental health
are inextricably linked.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.760
And everybody knows this from a kind of

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:26.340
locational experience.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.310
Pollution is something
that we have known for

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.530
hundreds of years, and
we know that pollution

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:35.890
and environmental condition
clearly affects human health.

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:39.100
But over time, we have
also begun to realize that

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.070
it's not just a locational issue,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.870
it used to be an issue of
urban geography to those of you

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.330
who maybe have studied
geography in your lives,

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.910
where if you look particularly
in the United Kingdom

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:52.940
at the settlement patterns
and urbanization patterns

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:57.940
the wealthy always lived up
wind of an industrial factory

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.300
or industrial infrastructure.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.120
And the poor lived down wind.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:06.873
Already there you saw the
impact of pollution, inequality,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.640
and quality of life, including
the impact on the health

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.590
of people, very clearly
manifested in a way that

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:16.610
today really has taken
on a planetary dimension.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:18.340
And if I may just fast
forward a little bit

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:20.820
and take another example of pollution

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:25.490
that already 40 years ago
began to preoccupy us.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:30.030
Ozone depleting substances,
destroying a very thin film

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:34.300
of ozone, the ozone layer
around our planet earth.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.380
And our recognition that
we had to phase out these

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:40.220
ozone depleting substances
in order to avoid

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.300
what was already
beginning to be manifestly

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:46.010
a planetary health problem,
a hole in the ozone layer,

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:49.790
increased number in cancer,
skin cancers, premature death.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:54.450
And so the toll, the numbers
and the empirical evidence

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.610
continue to mount about how environmental,

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.660
not just destruction, but
also environmental impacts,

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.450
clearly were linked to human wellbeing.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.590
Fast-forward a little bit,
because this is a sort of

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:08.210
common sense entry point,
but today we live in a time

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.550
and Professor Nelson just alluded to it,

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.530
We have taken this notion
of living in the age

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.850
of the Anthropocene as a way
of trying to re conceptualize

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.550
quite how significant
the impact of humans is.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.800
Anthropocene, the age of
humans is a simple way

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.800
of expressing an immensely
complex set of changes

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.740
and developments that are underway.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.540
But using the term
Anthropocene, and it is not yet

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:36.470
an accepted one in the geological
sense of an age or an era

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.390
like the Holocene, but
scientists and bodies

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.870
are currently debating
whether indeed the notion

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:44.550
of living in the age of the
Anthropocene after the Holocene

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.490
does make sense.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.390
And what it really implies is two things.

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:52.130
One, the nature and scale
of the impact of humans

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.500
on the planet has become
geologically traceable.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:57.760
We are now the dominant
species on this planet.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.730
We are changing its
atmosphere, its biosphere.

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:04.530
We are changing the composition
of the quality of water,

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.360
the quality of air,
and many other elements

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.210
that signals to us that
this is a qualitative

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:13.160
and quantitative different
dimension in which we need

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.890
to think about the
presence of homo sapiens,

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:18.780
the human species so
to speak on the planet.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:21.360
And to some this is obviously
very much a narrative

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.050
associated with the doomsday scenario,

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:26.750
but let me very quickly try
and counter that notion.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:30.730
Yes, it is a way of understanding
quite how significant

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:34.520
our collective impact
is on this planet today

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:36.410
and by implication and by consequences

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.340
on future generations.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.200
But it is also a way of trying
to focus on the fact that

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.610
we have choices.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:46.350
Never in human history before
have we had the wealth,

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:48.580
the science, the
knowledge, the technology,

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.950
the possibilities of
choosing different pathways

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.560
and therefore living in
the age of the Anthropocene

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.080
is not inevitably a doomsday scenario,

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.960
on the contrary, it is an era of choices

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.840
and choices with consequences
that are intergenerational.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:06.270
Now very quickly, fast forward
to how we have measured

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:07.103
human progress.

00:11:07.103 --> 00:11:10.080
Because part of the planetary
health notion, people, planet,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.900
prosperity, has basically
being this very simplistic way

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.500
that essentially its through
economics, through money,

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.110
through wealth, through
gross domestic product,

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:22.260
per capita GDP, that we
have spent the better part

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:26.300
of 100 years of measuring human
progress, human development.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.570
And it was that led in about 30 years ago,

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.650
UNDP to challenge this
notion that the best way

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.150
to measure progress and
development was to do so

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:37.490
through this economic narrow lens.

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.340
That's when the Human Development
Report was first conceived

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:44.920
of by very inspiring and
forward looking economists,

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:48.850
Professor Amartya Sen, Mahbub
ul Haq and many others

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:50.540
who came up with the
human development index.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:53.036
And in its simplest form,
it was a way of expanding

00:11:53.036 --> 00:11:56.390
the lens, introducing health and education

00:11:56.390 --> 00:11:59.450
as additional parameters
for measuring human progress

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:02.840
and very deeply rooted in
the notion of capabilities,

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:06.730
capabilities to succeed as a human being.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:10.500
Now, we still as a world,
continued to measure progress

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.910
almost exclusively through this GDP lens.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.270
And it is one that has led us
essentially to the point where

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:21.270
today you could argue we
are truly missing the point,

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:24.990
namely that people, planet and prosperity,

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.770
have a far more intricate relationship

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:30.460
and decisions need to evolve
far more rapidly in order to

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:34.440
ensure sure that human
wellbeing and the corollary

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:37.420
of planetary wellbeing,
of planetary health

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:38.760
need to be managed in sync.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.420
We need to measure progress differently.

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:42.540
We need to take different decisions.

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:47.500
And this is why in 2020, our
Human Development Report shows

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.330
the new frontier, the next
frontier in human development

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.760
as being related precisely to this issue.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.890
We introduced a new planet readjusted

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:57.810
human development index
that try to capture

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:00.600
the health of the planet
through the impacts that

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.540
we are having as humans
combined with a traditional

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.170
human development index.

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:06.470
It's an experimental index.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:08.370
We have put it out there
for the world to consider

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:11.450
what we believe it is perhaps
the best way in which we can

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:15.690
currently give expression to
this notion planetary health

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:17.363
and it's links to human health.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:21.280
Now and its consequences,
and I'll end with that,

00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:23.760
is a simple recognition, and
Bobby Kennedy once put it

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:26.780
very simply, he says that, "GDP
measures everything in short

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:30.290
except that which makes life worthwhile."

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:34.200
And it is that search for
a way in which we can take

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:38.020
a common sense realization,
an intuitive understanding

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:40.670
that underpins that
notion of planetary health

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:44.160
and translate it into measurable
and empirically validated

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:44.993
foundations.

00:13:44.993 --> 00:13:46.510
Because that is how we make public policy.

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:49.860
That is how we trade off
choices in different contexts.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:53.830
And much of what UNDP does
today, not least in the midst

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:58.550
of a COVID-19 crisis, is to
help countries conceive of

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:00.580
a development pathway out of this crisis,

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:02.400
building forward better.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.650
Now it's easily proclaimed.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:06.270
It is easily stated.

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.120
How do you do it?

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.500
You have to make policy choices about

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:12.110
the fiscal policy
systems, taxation systems,

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.060
because here is the first distortion,

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:16.580
our markets are not operating rationally.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:18.440
They subsidize things that harm us

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.490
and essentially harm ultimately prosperity

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:25.730
and tax the good decisions
that people take.

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:29.760
This is the taxation system
of the early 21st century.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.530
We tax labor through high income taxes

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:34.053
but we hardly tax pollution.

00:14:34.053 --> 00:14:36.700
It's not rational in this age.

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:39.170
And it is fundamental to
understanding why we have come

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:41.730
to a point where planetary
health has become

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:43.230
such a challenge.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:46.070
We need to look also at
the way that inequality

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:48.560
intersects with planetary health.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.070
The fact that research
showed us some years ago

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:54.740
that when lead was still
being put into petrol

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:58.710
with the argument that
essentially that was a price

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:01.630
we had to pay for affordable mobility,

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.150
it was the poor who lived by the roadsides

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.290
of the main highways that
were most effected by lead

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:08.210
in petrol.

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.130
And it was the children
of poor people who had far

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:15.510
higher levels of lead in
their bodies than children

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:16.640
of wealthier families.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.020
Because they lived further
away from the highways.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:21.660
They had gardens, they
lived in leafier suburbs.

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:25.000
Simple illustrations of how inequality

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.930
and environmental impacts
and human health interface.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:31.490
Let me end by just saying,
'cause I look forward very much

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:34.010
to the questions and the
discussions that we have,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.483
that in a world of the 21st
century, in thinking about

00:15:36.483 --> 00:15:39.120
the future of development,
which is so much the focus

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.560
of UNDP's work today and that
of the United Nations system

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:47.510
in helping a very unequal
world and a very stressed world

00:15:48.401 --> 00:15:50.450
to find together a better way forward,

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:52.700
using the Sustainable Development
Goals that many of you

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:56.150
will have heard, not as
some metric, but rather

00:15:56.150 --> 00:16:00.650
as a compass on which we can
together address a reset,

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:04.130
a recalibration of the
yardsticks of human progress.

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:09.080
And ultimately enable us to
realize a very simple fact,

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:13.860
prosperity is about people
and not about money.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:16.020
We measure prosperity in monetary terms

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:18.150
but actually what we really mean is,

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:22.530
are people able to fulfill
their aspirations in life?

00:16:22.530 --> 00:16:24.590
And so to demonetize to some extent,

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:26.730
not to ignore that dimension
but to demonetize it

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:30.260
to some extent and to compliment
it with an understanding

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:33.740
of how much people therefore
also depend on the planet

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:37.960
for their wellbeing and
how the way we consume

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:41.160
and produce and exercise
our economic choices,

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:45.470
ultimately shapes the nature of our planet

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:47.730
and its ability to sustain us,

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:52.530
is at the heart of rethinking
people, planet and prosperity

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:53.720
in our age.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:54.553
Thank you so much.

00:16:54.553 --> 00:16:56.050
And President Nelson back to you.

00:16:58.680 --> 00:16:59.513
- Hi.

00:16:59.513 --> 00:17:01.660
So actually this is Robert Dubrow,

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:02.893
so it's back to me.

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:04.900
So yeah.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.240
Thanks Administrators
Steiner for some very

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:09.683
insightful comments.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.810
And building on those comments,

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:14.420
I wanted to ask the first question.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:19.420
So I and Dr. Nelson will
ask a couple of questions

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:22.510
and then we're gonna open
it up to the audience.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:26.770
So my first question is, and
you you dealt to some degree

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:27.610
with this already.

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:29.943
So I was wondering if you could elaborate,

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:34.740
what is the scope and scale of
human pressures on the planet

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:38.950
and importantly do the social
imbalances and inequality

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:40.553
contribute to the problem?

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:47.180
- Well, I gave you a couple
of parameters already,

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:51.630
but just to give you a
sense, let me, for instance

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:56.630
give you a couple of more
indicators of the scale

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:58.220
for example.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.810
If you realize that the
total mass of things

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:04.100
humans have made like
buildings, roads, bottle tops,

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:08.440
plastic bags now exceeds the
total mass of all living things

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.000
on our planet.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.830
It gives you one sense of
the anthropogenic impact

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:16.740
we have had or this sheer
scale of what we have.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:19.070
And there are some
wonderful other analogies

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:20.460
about chicken bones for example.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.540
A few people will
realize that chickens are

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:26.610
the most mass produced,
consumable livestock in the world.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.470
And if you were to add
up all the chicken bones

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:31.990
ever consumed, extraordinary
mountains would be formed

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:33.043
on planet earth.

00:18:33.914 --> 00:18:38.120
But let me also give you
a sense of quiet how dark

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:40.080
some of these statistics
have become particularly

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.230
in the last 100 years.

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:45.120
We have destroyed well over
50% of the world's wetlands

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:48.370
in just half a century,
in the name of progress,

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:50.470
in the name of development, in the name of

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:55.490
useless environmental assets
that are not being put into

00:18:55.490 --> 00:18:56.940
productive use.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:18:59.750
We have dammed most of the
world's rivers with the argument

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:02.630
that the ingenuity, human
ingenuity of building dams

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:07.110
to produce electricity,
irrigate lands always outweighs

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:09.870
the ingenuity of nature
that has given us these

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:14.040
extraordinary riverine and
hydrological ecosystems

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:17.120
that have such extraordinary functions

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.960
in our planetary context,
but also in terms of

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:22.760
humanity's survival.

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:27.330
And water is another very
good example and metaphor

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:29.340
through which to look at
the kind of situations

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.350
we have created for ourselves.

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:33.950
In a country like Australia
today, the Murray-Darling basin

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:37.750
is a river basin where most
of the water is now traded.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.880
It is traded and it is no
longer used for farming

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:43.890
but in fact, farmers
trade their water rights

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:47.650
to the urban city municipalities
in order to ensure

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:49.880
that these municipalities
have enough water to

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:53.930
provide drinking water
to the urban population.

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:57.040
We are reaching in so
many aspects and also

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.170
parts of the world, planetary boundaries.

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:03.000
It's a concept that has
emerged in recent years.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:04.940
And I think provides us
with a very useful way

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:08.600
of understanding what is
the boundary condition

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:10.340
after which you're no longer dealing

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:14.090
with the planets ingenious
systems of producing what we need

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:17.060
to survive in it, but you are
depleting the capital stock,

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:19.830
so to speak and you're
destroying the capability

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.890
of nature to sustain us.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:25.360
And another horrendous
statistic is that roughly 70%

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.410
seven, zero of the world's
wildlife may have been lost

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:31.340
in the past 50 years.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:34.750
Add to that climate change that
we are just a few years away

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:38.170
from passing the point at which
carbon emissions accumulated

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:42.130
in the atmosphere are going
to move beyond the point

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:46.493
of no return in terms of a
1.5 degree warming scenario.

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.670
For generations, probably
hundreds of years,

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:52.640
people will no longer have
even the option of trying to

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:55.160
get back to a world that maybe has warming

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:56.950
below 1.5 degrees.

00:20:56.950 --> 00:21:01.950
No, it is then essentially
a reality out of control.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:04.720
These are some of the
parameters within which I think

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:09.027
we can begin to comprehend
the scope and scale of change.

00:21:09.027 --> 00:21:12.800
And that is why I often say
we are the first generation

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:16.390
of the Anthropocene, where truly
we have began to understand

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:20.090
that we are changing the
atmosphere and the biosphere

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:23.880
on this planet, inconceivable
to our grandparents,

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:28.023
a reality and a very stark
one to our children already.

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:33.930
- Administrator Steiner,
I wasn't prepared for that

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:35.830
chicken bone example.

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:38.310
I have to say, I feel
personally responsible

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:39.810
for that particular challenge.

00:21:40.733 --> 00:21:44.777
- You have your work cut out.

00:21:44.777 --> 00:21:47.710
- In the Human Development 2020 Report,

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:52.710
it was stark how it laid out,
how calamities are really

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:57.490
the product of this concept
of people and planet

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:58.613
being out of sync.

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:03.920
Talk to us about how
COVID-19 is an example

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:07.170
of this imbalance between
human and planetary health

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:09.990
and also what have we learned from it

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:14.163
and how can we be prepared for
the next pandemic, calamity?

00:22:16.555 --> 00:22:19.550
- You know to me personally,
the pandemic has been

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:23.080
a stark reminder of something
that maybe 20 years ago

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:28.080
really led me to focus on
the link between environment

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:31.900
and development or environment
and human wellbeing

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:35.850
in ways that maybe when I was
studying at the university,

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.430
as a development economist,
had not really been

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:39.893
at the forefront of my mind.

00:22:41.207 --> 00:22:44.620
And that was the realization
that in so many ways,

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:49.330
at this point in time, we
are confronted with choices

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:54.330
that yes, in some respects
still must be based

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.430
on imperfect knowledge.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:58.400
But this is where I sometimes
have often struggled

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.000
with the debates about climate change.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.930
Because there's this notion
that we will only decide

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:06.100
to do something when we
have perfect knowledge,

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.270
goes against everything
that we have learned

00:23:08.270 --> 00:23:09.660
in human history.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:12.350
And who ever in life has
the privilege of having

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:16.410
perfect knowledge and foresight
in order to take a decision?

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:19.710
And I think it is that conundrum
in which we find ourselves

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:23.850
namely the necessity to act
with sufficient information

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:28.022
to make us more cautionary
about what comes next

00:23:28.022 --> 00:23:31.760
and yet not having a guarantee
that the solutions we have

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:32.810
will work.

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:35.870
COVID-19 first of all is
a perfect illustration

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:37.140
of the latter.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:40.300
So many decisions have to
be taken in a number of days

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:42.160
so often we got it wrong.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:45.314
So often we paid a price
which in hindsight you ask

00:23:45.314 --> 00:23:46.950
yourself, "how could this have happened?"

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:50.300
The second big lesson is, I
think we need to realize that

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:52.800
preparedness is not a luxury.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:56.384
And if you just take the numbers today,

00:23:56.384 --> 00:24:01.384
the cost of this pandemic alone
is about 500 times as much

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:06.530
as a pandemic prevention
measures combination

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:08.980
would have cost us if we
had taken them in time.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:12.450
And probably that number
is an underestimate.

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:14.970
Because we did not take
this thing seriously

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:17.510
despite the fact that we
had SARS, we had Ebola,

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:22.510
we had enough illustrations
about how zoonotic diseases

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:27.110
can jump from the animal
kingdom so to speak,

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:29.520
into human bodies.

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:31.810
This is not something that has
happened for the first time

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:36.080
to us, it has happened to us
time and again in history.

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.700
But now we are in the 21st century.

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:41.230
And here, I think there is
the added dimension that with

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:45.250
travel, with global trade, but
also with the interdependence

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:48.550
that we have built up, we
are so much more vulnerable,

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:52.140
physically, economically and socially.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:56.170
And I think it is this
combination of realities

00:24:56.170 --> 00:25:00.470
that I think is making us
all reflect far more deeply

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:04.840
about what will life be
like following COVID-19.

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:06.580
And let's always remind
ourselves that we are still

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:08.810
in the midst of this crisis.

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:11.320
Yes, some countries are now
vaccinating ahead of others

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:15.630
but just the vaccination dilemma
is already an illustration

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:18.370
of how difficult we're finding it to act

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:20.940
as a global community, when
we know that we're gonna

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:22.460
lose this battle against the virus

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:24.350
if we cannot vaccinate everyone.

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:28.120
And yet look at the statistical
reality of who is getting

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:30.720
vaccinated right now and who is not.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:34.210
This is one of the
illustrations of what we mean by

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:35.830
acting against our own interests.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:38.710
This is not rational, and this
is the price we are paying

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:41.530
for not taking the notion
of resilience seriously.

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:45.870
And the relationship between
nature and human wellbeing

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:50.450
is not only, I think illustrated
in this instance by a virus

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:54.230
that has moved into our
lives in such a brutal way,

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:56.830
it is the realization that
so much of what has happened

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.040
in the last 50 to 100 years in particular,

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:02.050
because that's where all the
curves went up exponentially,

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:05.980
deforestation, pollution,
destruction of wetlands,

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:09.940
et cetera, soil fertility
that is being lost,

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:11.950
ocean acidification.

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:16.950
These are all drivers of
greater environmental change

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:19.610
and ultimately vulnerability.

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:21.730
And I think the more we
are able to see these

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:24.570
systems interact, the more we recognize

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:26.890
that we are actually
entering into an age of

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:29.780
extreme vulnerability and even more so

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.100
of a lack of preparedness.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:35.770
And therefore resilience
becomes more elusive

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:38.590
rather than something that we have built.

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:42.600
At a moment in time when
science technology and wealth

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:45.413
for that matter, would
have suggested otherwise.

00:26:48.156 --> 00:26:50.553
- Yeah, thank you, Administrator Steiner.

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:56.580
I wanted to turn to how
we actually effect change.

00:26:57.350 --> 00:27:01.740
So the question is how can
we encourage policy makers

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:06.150
and legislators to consider the
impacts of planetary health?

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:10.730
How can we incentivize
positive change and innovations

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:15.340
at all levels, at the local
domestic, regional and global?

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:18.380
- Again, at the risk
of over simplification,

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:22.633
but as a first starter to
respond to your question.

00:27:23.610 --> 00:27:27.370
I think there are a number
of drivers that ultimately

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:31.930
will determine how a
society makes choices.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:35.710
And the first one has to be
informed and empowered citizens.

00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:38.020
I think whether even if
you're living in a more

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:42.400
autocratic regime or you're
living in the ideal democracy,

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:45.160
which probably is also
an illusive aspiration

00:27:45.160 --> 00:27:46.610
perhaps in the minds of many,

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:52.340
informed and empowered citizens
are ultimately the base

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:56.440
upon which you can both
transact and develop

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:58.790
different policy choices.

00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:02.080
And I think one of the great
things about time is that

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:05.480
if you, even if I look
at the generations that

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:10.410
are now coming after us, kids
who are five, 10, 15 year old

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:11.680
today,

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:14.670
they live in an age where
information and the ability

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:17.240
to understand more about what
is happening to the world,

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:20.240
not just around you, where
we absolutely grew up

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:23.840
still in a very isolated
family or rural community

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:27.060
might've found ourselves in
the sixties or even seventies,

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:30.250
today for many children around the world,

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:33.720
understanding that there
is a world out there

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:37.000
and that they are part of it,
come so much more naturally.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:40.140
And I think therein lies,
one of my reasons for being

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:42.810
an optimist, because I
do believe that informed

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:45.640
and empowered citizens do act differently.

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:50.640
And just take the example
of Fridays For Future.

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:54.848
And here we are in a moment
in time where 13, 14,

00:28:54.848 --> 00:28:58.900
15 year olds have essentially taken charge

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:03.308
of the most central challenge
of our time, climate change.

00:29:03.308 --> 00:29:07.750
Not only has it opened the
eyes and opened conversations

00:29:07.750 --> 00:29:10.720
in virtually every country
in a different way,

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:12.040
they are leading the debate,

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:14.240
and they are leading it
with a clarity of purpose

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:17.710
and understanding that
I must say I'm sometimes

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:20.790
envious when I compare my daily life,

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:23.210
sitting in the United Nations conferences

00:29:23.210 --> 00:29:26.620
and looking at how they
sometimes try to transact

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:28.760
despite what we know.

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.320
But this is, the second part.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:34.650
And herein lies, I think
a critical understanding,

00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:38.750
our economies are foundation
to what sustains us.

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:42.680
It drives our ability to
ultimately have employment,

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:46.470
have food, access to electricity,
healthcare, and so on.

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:51.130
So how our economy sends
signals and in which direction,

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:54.770
it encourages consumption and
production or discourages it,

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:56.230
is critical.

00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:59.460
One taxation and fiscal policy,

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:02.880
we still live in an age where,
as I mentioned earlier on,

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:07.250
we subsidize things that
we know based on science

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:11.430
and evidence are harmful, but
because of legacy interests,

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:13.270
because of the inertia in our economies,

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:17.060
because of economic power,
being invested in perhaps

00:30:18.035 --> 00:30:20.640
a few have a lot to gain
from the status quo.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.280
We are not acting on knowledge.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:25.680
We are keeping an economic system going,

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:28.300
that essentially has to shift.

00:30:28.300 --> 00:30:30.250
Whether you call it a green economy,

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:34.970
a more equitable economy,
that social contract

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:39.930
in which governments essentially
shape the economic rules

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:41.970
of the game needs to evolve.

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:46.870
And fiscal policy, taxation,
fossil fuel subsidies,

00:30:46.870 --> 00:30:49.780
these are just some egregious examples

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:50.867
of where we need to focus on.

00:30:50.867 --> 00:30:54.630
And it ultimately comes down
to how financial markets

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:56.920
will view the future.

00:30:56.920 --> 00:31:00.950
Financial markets, I think are
often viewed as this sort of,

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:04.290
untamable dinosaur or monster.

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:06.780
But really financial
markets at their simplest

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:08.790
are fairly mercenary.

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:11.460
Where you can make money,
you will go and make money.

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:14.730
So what is it that makes
it so difficult for us

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:18.700
at this crucial moment in
time to also incentivize

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:21.630
financial markets to invest differently?

00:31:21.630 --> 00:31:24.230
To invest in tomorrow's
economy, rather than

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:25.270
in yesterday's economy.

00:31:25.270 --> 00:31:27.760
And here is the great debate of our time.

00:31:27.760 --> 00:31:30.560
How much does the state,
how do governments step in,

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:33.750
how much do governments also
compensate for the fact that

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:37.540
markets are not very good at transacting

00:31:37.540 --> 00:31:40.020
in the interest of the public good?

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:42.730
It's not that they are
automatically at cross purposes

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:45.650
with the public good, but
the public good clearly down

00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:47.860
right now, demands that we get to a zero,

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:50.610
net zero emissions world by 2050.

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:53.950
And there is no one who
has yet proven to me,

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:56.020
that markets left to their own devices,

00:31:56.020 --> 00:31:57.860
will do this in time.

00:31:57.860 --> 00:32:01.090
Which is why regulatory
and public policy signals

00:32:01.090 --> 00:32:03.630
need to accelerate the pace of change,

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:04.880
give rise to new markets.

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:07.260
And frankly speaking, we
are already seeing it happen

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.110
where we put the right measures in place.

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:10.290
Renewable energy.

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:12.930
We're in the midst of a
renewable energy revolution.

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:15.360
It is transforming our energy systems.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.630
And hundreds of years of
relying on fossil fuels

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:23.040
are probably, 20 to 30 years
down the line of becoming

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:25.920
really anachronism.

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:28.710
And that's how quickly we can shift,

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:30.730
for example on something
like fossil fuels,

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:34.120
the lifeblood of our 20th
century, 19th century,

00:32:34.120 --> 00:32:36.080
18th century economies.

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:40.140
And by 2050, I guarantee
you access to electricity,

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.830
to energy for most people on the planet,

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:45.100
will be far less of a
problem than it has ever been

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:46.570
in human history.

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:49.790
And that is the kind of
economic shift that we need.

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:53.640
So encouraging policy makers
and legislators begins

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:56.930
with citizens, empowered
and informed citizens.

00:32:56.930 --> 00:33:00.450
It then involves governments
and it recognizes how

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:04.150
central the rules of the
game of our markets are

00:33:04.150 --> 00:33:06.240
in driving change or preventing change

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:11.700
- Administrator Steiner, I
have to say your perspective

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:14.420
as an optimist definitely
comes through in the

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:15.650
Human Development Report.

00:33:15.650 --> 00:33:20.650
I recall the language
about humans as agents.

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:27.820
Humans, as people that have
the power and the capacity

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:32.110
to produce better for themselves.

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:34.950
And that the goal of human
development really is about

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:37.780
facilitating the conditions
to support human freedom

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:39.100
and autonomy.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:43.200
And as someone who leads
an agency that works with

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:46.760
170 countries around the
world for poverty eradication

00:33:46.760 --> 00:33:51.320
and inclusive society, I wanna ask you,

00:33:51.320 --> 00:33:54.820
how could we create strong
partnerships and collaborations

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:57.220
across the world to
fulfill the promise of the

00:33:57.220 --> 00:34:01.014
climate agenda while also as we face

00:34:01.014 --> 00:34:04.310
the socio-economic crises
that has been triggered

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:05.750
by the COVID-19 pandemic?

00:34:05.750 --> 00:34:07.300
What are your thoughts on that?

00:34:08.850 --> 00:34:10.950
- Kindly if you would allow
me just a brief remark

00:34:10.950 --> 00:34:15.940
on agency, I think it is
a very critical aspect of

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:18.453
the 2020 Human Development Report because,

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:23.206
it's very easy for many
of us in today's world,

00:34:23.206 --> 00:34:25.200
the fourth industrial revolution world,

00:34:25.200 --> 00:34:27.790
the digitalization world,
artificial intelligence,

00:34:27.790 --> 00:34:31.140
everybody talking about
systems complexity,

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:32.840
to feel this empowered.

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:37.670
And I think this is a very
dangerous narrative to pursue.

00:34:37.670 --> 00:34:40.040
And that's why in the
Human Development Report,

00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:44.660
we tried to put so much attention
on this notion of agency,

00:34:44.660 --> 00:34:49.050
the ability to choose, the
capabilities to make choices

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:51.700
that are different from
those that are simply handed

00:34:51.700 --> 00:34:53.070
down to us.

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:55.513
And choices really matter.

00:34:57.046 --> 00:35:01.600
And in a recent TED talk,
used the analogy that,

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:05.610
systems don't change systems,
people change systems.

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:08.500
And it is very much with that philosophy

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:13.330
or with that paradigm let's
say in mind, that we approach

00:35:13.330 --> 00:35:17.560
these important choices that
we have to make right now.

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:20.070
And you asked me about partnerships.

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:22.560
These is to answer this,
the more we work together,

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:23.560
the easier it will be.

00:35:23.560 --> 00:35:25.490
I mean, this is the foundational principle

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:27.090
of the United Nations.

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:30.610
in 1945 on the ashes of
the Second World War,

00:35:30.610 --> 00:35:34.410
and having learned the bitter
lesson of what happens to a

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:36.660
world that is capable of self destruction,

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:39.260
even more so after the Second World War,

00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:44.260
finding both the place,
the space and the ability

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:49.150
to work together despite our differences

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:51.950
clearly is at the heart of
any notion of partnership.

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:54.810
And I would begin when
we have a challenge such

00:35:54.810 --> 00:35:57.740
as climate change to remind ourselves that

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:01.610
with all the justified
criticism of 30 years

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:06.610
of climate negotiations,
from the Rio 1992 Summit

00:36:06.950 --> 00:36:10.070
through the Copenhagen
Climate Convention Summit

00:36:10.070 --> 00:36:12.020
into the Paris Agreement.

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:14.150
And yes, here we are in the year 2021

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:16.590
and time is still running out.

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:18.703
But at the same time,
just imagine for a moment

00:36:18.703 --> 00:36:22.730
that we didn't have the
IPCC over 30 years ago,

00:36:22.730 --> 00:36:27.090
that kind of rescued
science from the dictum of

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:30.730
national policy and national
interest that allows scientists

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:34.020
to speak to the world
independently about what we knew

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:35.660
and what we didn't know
about what was happening

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:37.558
to our planet.

00:36:37.558 --> 00:36:39.758
If you then think of
the climate convention,

00:36:40.616 --> 00:36:43.630
do you think we would have
been worse off or better off

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:47.610
if not every year for the
past I think it is now 25

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:51.440
or 26 years, the climate
convention essentially brought

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:54.240
all these countries around
the table once a year

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:57.470
in the limelight of
international attention

00:36:57.470 --> 00:37:00.440
and showed up the world's
nations and leaders

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:02.880
for what they were doing or not doing?

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:06.810
I think we reached Paris in 2015

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:11.810
because the public saw how
much was not being done.

00:37:12.610 --> 00:37:15.210
And so that partnership
uncomfortable as it is,

00:37:15.210 --> 00:37:18.570
even in a very political
world of a G2 world,

00:37:18.570 --> 00:37:22.005
China and the US or Europe and Africa,

00:37:22.005 --> 00:37:25.760
you can find many reasons
to look at the world being

00:37:25.760 --> 00:37:26.793
very divided.

00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:30.580
And yet we have a Paris Agreement,

00:37:30.580 --> 00:37:33.060
we have a pathway to
raising ambition levels

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:36.740
every five years and
hopefully a possibility

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:39.060
of aligning where the world is acting

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:41.920
with where science is
telling us since years,

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:43.350
we should have acted.

00:37:43.350 --> 00:37:46.380
So this is one way of
thinking about partnership.

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:48.910
The other one may be between public sector

00:37:48.910 --> 00:37:49.770
and private sector.

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:52.840
And frankly speaking
anybody who thinks that the

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:56.550
private sector does not or
is not needed as a partner

00:37:56.550 --> 00:38:00.100
in this enterprise of challenging,

00:38:00.100 --> 00:38:02.100
of addressing the challenge
of climate change,

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:05.970
loss of biodiversity, simply
overlooks the fact that most

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:09.490
of the things that happen in
our daily lives are transacted

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:13.120
not through governments, not
through public sector budgets,

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:15.680
but through the private sector.

00:38:15.680 --> 00:38:17.950
And the private sector also is not always

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:21.640
corporate multinational, giant companies.

00:38:21.640 --> 00:38:23.950
Private sector is the small scale farmer.

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:25.660
It's the corner shop owner.

00:38:25.660 --> 00:38:27.750
It's the startup enterprise.

00:38:27.750 --> 00:38:30.910
It's the people who produce
most of the world's food today.

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:34.040
Small scale farmers produce over 70%

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:36.280
of the food we consume on the planet.

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:38.670
So the private sector is
essentially that extraordinary

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:41.850
diverse domain that we
need to bring along,

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:45.090
that we need to incentivize
with our consumer choices,

00:38:45.090 --> 00:38:47.970
with our public policies, with our laws,

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:50.380
in order to be part of the solution,

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:51.730
rather than part of the problem.

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:55.990
And that is perhaps the
greatest shift in thinking

00:38:55.990 --> 00:38:56.840
of our time.

00:38:56.840 --> 00:39:00.090
Because remember Milton
Friedman and not so long ago,

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:05.027
that the cradle, the religion
of economic correctness was,

00:39:05.027 --> 00:39:06.900
"companies have to think
about their shareholders

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:08.070
and nothing else."

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:11.180
We have come a long way and
we have paid a heavy price

00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:13.560
for the time we took
to come this long way.

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:16.794
But I think we are in many
ways at a point in time

00:39:16.794 --> 00:39:20.040
where the markets of the future,
on the one hand will drive

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:23.330
new enterprises and
businesses and where the costs

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:25.590
of being rooted in the legacy economy

00:39:25.590 --> 00:39:29.662
will simply become prohibitive
and stranded assets suddenly

00:39:29.662 --> 00:39:31.320
become a very stark reality.

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:34.860
So that is perhaps a way
of answering partnerships

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:36.273
from multiple perspectives.

00:39:38.330 --> 00:39:39.600
- Thank you.

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:41.230
Yeah, thanks as Administrator Steiner.

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:44.810
So now we're getting some
questions from the audience.

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:46.960
So I have a question here.

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:49.043
Actually, you mentioned World War Two.

00:39:50.010 --> 00:39:52.770
So the question is, "I wonder
if Administrator Steiner

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:56.550
could talk about a fourth
P, the young people, planet

00:39:56.550 --> 00:39:59.087
and prosperity that of peace."

00:39:59.087 --> 00:40:01.850
"Could you elaborate on
the relationship between

00:40:01.850 --> 00:40:05.270
climate change and
conflict and the importance

00:40:05.270 --> 00:40:08.757
of building peace while also
addressing climate change?"

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:13.200
- A very appropriate question.

00:40:13.200 --> 00:40:15.400
And in fact, earlier
today I was co-chairing

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:17.010
with my colleague Filippo Grandi

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:19.420
and the Director General
of Humanitarian Affairs

00:40:19.420 --> 00:40:21.953
of the Syria Pledging Conference.

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:26.520
Here is a conflict 10 years
old, unimaginable suffering

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:27.460
and tragedy.

00:40:27.460 --> 00:40:29.840
It's spill over effects
into neighboring countries

00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:33.050
such as Lebanon, Jordan, and Turkey

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:36.970
who are hosting millions of refugees.

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:39.900
And at a moment in time
where their own economies

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:40.770
are imploding.

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:43.640
I mean, you have all seen
the news about Lebanon.

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:48.180
So here is one example
of how peace is not only

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:53.800
a humanitarian catastrophe,
it is also a major setback

00:40:53.800 --> 00:40:54.920
in developmental terms.

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:57.690
And the same applies for so
many countries in conflict.

00:40:57.690 --> 00:41:00.970
Yemen in which UNDP and
the United Nations family

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:02.920
has now been working
for a number of years,

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:06.730
almost seven years in
another protracted conflict.

00:41:06.730 --> 00:41:09.240
We estimate that Yemen
has lost somewhere between

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:13.910
20 to 25 years of its
development in these six years.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:16.560
Just to give you a sense of
the setback that that conflict

00:41:16.560 --> 00:41:17.630
causes.

00:41:17.630 --> 00:41:20.330
Now, many of us have heard,
and I think we'll not be

00:41:20.330 --> 00:41:25.057
surprised that if climate
change really begins to affect,

00:41:26.060 --> 00:41:30.250
many of the things that we take
for granted, when it rains,

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:33.070
when we're able to have
enough water to plant,

00:41:33.070 --> 00:41:37.300
floods, the other extreme, but
also temperature variations,

00:41:37.300 --> 00:41:40.530
sea level rise, extreme weather events.

00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:45.240
The sheer impact on people
and humanity and our economies

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:48.100
that we have seen so
often in recent years,

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:51.320
here in the United States, in
the Caribbean where sometimes

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:55.910
a 24 hour event can wipe
out 30% of your GDP.

00:41:55.910 --> 00:42:00.810
This kind of disruption
is absolutely anathema to

00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:02.900
peaceful societies and
peaceful development.

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:05.730
That doesn't mean that people
suddenly turn on one another

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:08.900
because of a disaster, but
if we allow these pressures

00:42:08.900 --> 00:42:12.150
to continue and if you
do not prepare for them,

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:17.150
then it is quite clear that we
will see many more conflicts

00:42:17.230 --> 00:42:20.100
arising whether it is upstream, downstream

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:22.720
water sharing countries on river basins.

00:42:22.720 --> 00:42:26.920
Whether it is pastoralists
who have to essentially change

00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:29.670
their annual migrations
with their livestock

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:33.290
and are increasingly clashing
resident farming communities.

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:35.920
Whether it is the waves of
migrants who essentially

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:39.460
seen no future in their
countries and at the risk

00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:42.440
of their own lives, moving
into other parts of the world,

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:44.570
whether it is central
America to North America,

00:42:44.570 --> 00:42:47.360
whether it is Africa to Europe.

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:51.370
These are the kinds of
disruptions that very quickly,

00:42:51.370 --> 00:42:53.810
when governance systems don't work well,

00:42:53.810 --> 00:42:57.180
when societies are ill
prepared, give rise to tensions,

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:00.430
to conflict, to
radicalization, to extremism

00:43:00.430 --> 00:43:02.790
and a very vicious circle unfolds.

00:43:02.790 --> 00:43:06.970
And I think another live
illustration of this drama

00:43:06.970 --> 00:43:09.140
is what is playing out in the Sahel region

00:43:09.140 --> 00:43:12.220
probably for the better part
of the last 20 to 30 years.

00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:15.600
And it should look forward
unless we truly step in

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:18.300
and get to the root
causes of this disruption.

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:21.650
We will see a great deal, more conflict

00:43:21.650 --> 00:43:23.610
and the absence of peace.

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:27.600
So it is in my mind, very
appropriate that we are seeing

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:30.900
increasingly in the United
Nations, the link established

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:35.080
between climate change,
broader environmental change

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:37.730
and peace and security.

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:41.060
Environmental change is
threatening international

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:42.470
or human security.

00:43:42.470 --> 00:43:44.640
There is no doubt in my mind.

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:48.340
The question is, can we
find in the 21st century

00:43:48.340 --> 00:43:51.330
collective response that
is not simply rooted

00:43:51.330 --> 00:43:56.330
in 20th century or simply
geopolitical conflict

00:43:56.620 --> 00:43:58.320
and competitions?

00:43:58.320 --> 00:44:01.890
If that is the driving force,
then indeed you will continue

00:44:01.890 --> 00:44:04.320
to witness what you often see right now

00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:07.970
in the security council
in international fora,

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:11.870
the inability of governments to provide

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:13.903
collective leadership and partnership.

00:44:16.070 --> 00:44:17.000
- Thank you.

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:20.840
We have actually many more
questions in the Q&amp;A chat,

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:23.130
and we're gonna be able
to pose to you to answer.

00:44:23.130 --> 00:44:26.660
Administrator Steiner, so
professor Dubrow and I will select

00:44:26.660 --> 00:44:30.120
ones that we think will be
most interesting for you.

00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:31.927
So here's the next one.

00:44:31.927 --> 00:44:34.460
"Will you talk a little bit
more about the intersection

00:44:34.460 --> 00:44:37.240
of environmental justice
and planetary health

00:44:37.240 --> 00:44:40.630
with other systems of oppression,
that disproportionately

00:44:40.630 --> 00:44:44.620
impact identity groups,
for example the plight of

00:44:44.620 --> 00:44:47.170
Gullah Geechee land sustainability in the

00:44:47.170 --> 00:44:48.990
Gullah Geechee quarter
of the United States.

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:53.600
The Gullah Geechee are people
who are descendants of slaves,

00:44:53.600 --> 00:44:58.310
who've lived along the South
Carolina and South Georgia

00:44:58.310 --> 00:44:59.143
coastal areas.

00:44:59.143 --> 00:45:01.100
And I think that land is,

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:04.110
there's trouble with seizing
the land for development.

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:06.470
So this person is asking,

00:45:06.470 --> 00:45:09.690
can you talk a little bit
more about how those justice

00:45:09.690 --> 00:45:11.640
and planetary health systems intersect?

00:45:13.960 --> 00:45:14.793
- Thank you for this question.

00:45:14.793 --> 00:45:19.783
That is one that takes up a
significant amount of time

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:24.720
in my thinking that I think
of many of us who work in UNDP

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:29.690
because whether it is
through the reality of race,

00:45:29.690 --> 00:45:32.580
whether it is through poverty and wealth,

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:35.490
whether it is through the gender lens,

00:45:35.490 --> 00:45:40.490
inequalities amplify the
impact of the kinds of changes,

00:45:41.310 --> 00:45:43.670
environmental changes and
impacts that I have described.

00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:47.380
I earlier mentioned just
the the example of lead

00:45:47.380 --> 00:45:51.170
and air pollution and how,
just by virtue of your income,

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:53.740
your children very often
would be worse effected

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:55.720
because of where you have to live.

00:45:55.720 --> 00:46:00.720
Now race clearly introduces
in many societies,

00:46:01.130 --> 00:46:05.070
another dimension of either discrimination

00:46:05.070 --> 00:46:08.620
or simply of inadvertently
being more vulnerable,

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:12.010
more exposed to the impacts of change.

00:46:12.010 --> 00:46:16.340
Now, I don't know the particular
example that the question

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:21.340
raised just now, but clearly
race has played a part

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:23.383
in the United States for instance,

00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:27.890
most dramatically
illustrated during COVID-19.

00:46:27.890 --> 00:46:31.050
But if you also look at
the basic health data

00:46:31.050 --> 00:46:34.660
and health statistics
across countries such as

00:46:34.660 --> 00:46:38.180
the United States, but indeed
other countries as well,

00:46:38.180 --> 00:46:41.160
more often than not, there is a parameter,

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:45.550
there is a pattern in which
racial differentiation

00:46:45.550 --> 00:46:48.950
allows you to recognize
very differential impacts.

00:46:48.950 --> 00:46:52.810
And so I think understanding
the environmental impacts,

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:56.370
which are often transacted in
terms of scientific indicators

00:46:56.370 --> 00:47:00.080
and indices, they need
to be complimented by

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:05.840
let's say more social criteria,
because it allows us to see,

00:47:05.840 --> 00:47:09.360
not just the physicality of
what we're talking about,

00:47:09.360 --> 00:47:11.260
but the social reality.

00:47:11.260 --> 00:47:14.390
And that's where the notion
of justice becomes so real.

00:47:14.390 --> 00:47:17.280
Now, justice is involved in many ways.

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:20.140
There is injustice that is
associated with inequality.

00:47:20.140 --> 00:47:23.140
There is injustice that is discrimination

00:47:23.140 --> 00:47:26.240
and there is injustice
simply with negligence

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:28.940
that is often associated
with the way that,

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:30.623
governments and societies,

00:47:32.430 --> 00:47:35.410
well, at its simplest really
care about all of their

00:47:35.410 --> 00:47:36.940
citizens.

00:47:36.940 --> 00:47:38.513
Look at our world today.

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:43.580
Access to vaccines is
primarily and principally

00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:46.490
at this moment in time in the year 2021

00:47:46.490 --> 00:47:50.180
a function of how much money
you have, or you do not have.

00:47:50.180 --> 00:47:55.070
This is the 21st century in
which we are, still imprisoned

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:59.750
by such a crude way of
dealing with something

00:47:59.750 --> 00:48:02.230
that fundamentally offends
our sense of justice

00:48:02.230 --> 00:48:03.490
and fairness.

00:48:03.490 --> 00:48:06.433
Now you can take it very
quickly to climate justice.

00:48:07.670 --> 00:48:11.440
For example, speaking in
an intergenerational sense

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:13.940
I think to me, what
Fridays For Future did more

00:48:13.940 --> 00:48:18.660
than anything else, was to
remind me of how privileged I am

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:20.610
that I still have the
choice to do something

00:48:20.610 --> 00:48:24.070
about climate change,
while perhaps my sons

00:48:24.070 --> 00:48:28.360
or their children will not
even have the choice anymore.

00:48:28.360 --> 00:48:33.160
So here is a fundamental
issue of justice that again,

00:48:33.160 --> 00:48:35.410
I think defines the way
we need to understand

00:48:35.410 --> 00:48:37.690
our responsibility to act.

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:39.535
And then there is just transitional also.

00:48:39.535 --> 00:48:42.690
Let me give you a dilemma
that just a few weeks ago

00:48:42.690 --> 00:48:45.590
I was addressing with a vice president

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:48.930
and the advisory council
of an African nation

00:48:48.930 --> 00:48:51.340
which was basically trying
to look at how to build

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:53.200
forward better from COVID-19.

00:48:53.200 --> 00:48:55.570
And it's a country that
has discovered oil,

00:48:55.570 --> 00:48:58.710
that has still gas reserves,
and that essentially pose

00:48:58.710 --> 00:48:59.543
the question to me,

00:48:59.543 --> 00:49:02.907
"so where is the just transition for us?"

00:49:02.907 --> 00:49:07.907
"Because for us not drilling
and not exporting these

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:12.520
fossil fuel resources
is almost unexplainable

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:14.040
to our citizens."

00:49:14.040 --> 00:49:15.700
So if we were to take a step back,

00:49:15.700 --> 00:49:18.250
if he were to actually take a leap forward

00:49:18.250 --> 00:49:20.790
into the green economy,
invest in renewables

00:49:20.790 --> 00:49:25.790
and perhaps not exploit 50%
of our fossil fuel reserves,

00:49:26.490 --> 00:49:29.190
where's the justice in
that when essentially

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:31.620
industrialized world has spent 200 years

00:49:31.620 --> 00:49:34.180
and the United States just
in the last four years

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:36.570
was the world's largest
producer of oil and gas.

00:49:36.570 --> 00:49:38.470
I mean let us remind ourselves,

00:49:38.470 --> 00:49:41.960
the largest carbon dioxide
emitter over the last 200 years

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:46.100
taken collectively, cumulatively,
also was the country

00:49:46.100 --> 00:49:50.490
that became the largest oil
producer in the last four years.

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:52.650
So if you are a small African economy,

00:49:52.650 --> 00:49:55.820
where is the just transition
that was promised as part

00:49:55.820 --> 00:50:00.560
of this global climate policy
in this world of today?

00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:03.840
And this is why and what
we do in the United Nations

00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:06.655
whether it's the convention,
whether it's UNDP

00:50:06.655 --> 00:50:09.300
whether it's the United
Nations Environment Program,

00:50:09.300 --> 00:50:12.300
we try to address this
injustice by finding ways

00:50:12.300 --> 00:50:15.200
in which a more just
transition becomes possible.

00:50:15.200 --> 00:50:17.850
And the same can be
extrapolated right back

00:50:17.850 --> 00:50:19.430
to a local community.

00:50:19.430 --> 00:50:22.880
If you have grown up in
a coal mining community

00:50:22.880 --> 00:50:26.510
and tomorrow, somebody
tells you, "it's over."

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:28.230
You're not just dealing
with shutting a mine,

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:31.420
you may find, 20,000 jobs for the people,

00:50:31.420 --> 00:50:34.440
you're essentially killing
a community in the way that

00:50:34.440 --> 00:50:39.260
it has seen and identified
itself for decades sometimes.

00:50:39.260 --> 00:50:42.010
It's not just money
that buys justice here,

00:50:42.010 --> 00:50:47.010
it's a different sense of what
a just transition could mean

00:50:47.230 --> 00:50:48.560
to that community.

00:50:48.560 --> 00:50:51.040
So let me stop here but
I think it's a topic

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:54.830
that is extremely important
and if we fail or succeed

00:50:54.830 --> 00:50:58.520
in really meeting the climate challenge,

00:50:58.520 --> 00:51:01.170
I think it will largely
be as a result of finding

00:51:01.170 --> 00:51:03.617
the right answers to this issue, namely,

00:51:03.617 --> 00:51:05.277
"what is a just transition."

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:09.203
- Thank you.

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:12.110
- Yeah, thanks Administrator Steiner.

00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:15.700
So we have time for one more question

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:17.683
and probably a fairly short answer.

00:51:18.610 --> 00:51:23.610
So the question is with the
goal of enabling ordinary people

00:51:24.020 --> 00:51:27.010
to realize their individual
and personal aspirations

00:51:27.010 --> 00:51:30.780
themselves, how can
science be made more useful

00:51:31.951 --> 00:51:34.493
as a tool for use by ordinary people?

00:51:37.528 --> 00:51:39.400
- Now, as somebody who has
never brilliant at school

00:51:39.400 --> 00:51:41.870
in the sciences, I'm not
sure I'm the best person

00:51:41.870 --> 00:51:43.040
to answer that question.

00:51:43.040 --> 00:51:48.040
But let me perhaps share with
you a simple lesson in life.

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:52.370
And, since I now lead an
organization that has 18,000 people

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:55.750
to who I'm answerable, to
who I also have to sound

00:51:55.750 --> 00:51:57.670
intelligent enough to be
able to listen to good

00:51:57.670 --> 00:51:58.913
scientific advice.

00:51:59.860 --> 00:52:02.463
I think we need to recognize that,

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:06.800
in one person you will
never find everything.

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:09.940
Whether it is scientific
knowledge or whether it is wisdom

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:12.530
or whether it is
perspective for that matter.

00:52:12.530 --> 00:52:14.800
It is how you bring people together

00:52:14.800 --> 00:52:16.640
and how you make collective thinking,

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:19.120
collective intelligence work.

00:52:19.120 --> 00:52:22.700
And how you're able to listen
to science, not as a dictate,

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:26.840
because science also really
doesn't answer questions.

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:30.910
It provides you input that
information in order for us to do

00:52:30.910 --> 00:52:34.828
what makes us as a human
species, so unique.

00:52:34.828 --> 00:52:38.950
We can process information,
we can essentially

00:52:38.950 --> 00:52:43.387
assimilate facts and then we make choices.

00:52:43.387 --> 00:52:46.270
We can make judgment calls.

00:52:46.270 --> 00:52:50.620
Now, sometimes those calls
are almost mathematical

00:52:50.620 --> 00:52:51.930
and the answer is very simple.

00:52:51.930 --> 00:52:53.373
Two plus two is four.

00:52:54.270 --> 00:52:58.850
But most of the time in
life, I think the thing that

00:52:58.850 --> 00:53:01.940
I have learned is that
science provides me with

00:53:01.940 --> 00:53:03.690
a reference point.

00:53:03.690 --> 00:53:06.050
Sometimes, yes, if it's antibiotics,

00:53:06.050 --> 00:53:09.000
it's also a very simple treatment.

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:11.560
But for most of the things
that we are trying to deal with

00:53:11.560 --> 00:53:14.273
in terms of sustainable development,

00:53:14.273 --> 00:53:18.350
the future of development in
this age of the Anthropocene,

00:53:18.350 --> 00:53:20.620
science is only one dimension of it.

00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:25.220
And I think we have to accept that,

00:53:25.220 --> 00:53:29.030
science is a field that is
constantly growing exponentially

00:53:29.030 --> 00:53:31.820
just like with digitalization
and technology,

00:53:31.820 --> 00:53:35.850
and therefore specialization
is critical and not all of us,

00:53:35.850 --> 00:53:38.620
most of us can be those specialists.

00:53:38.620 --> 00:53:41.410
So the question is how
do we create processes

00:53:41.410 --> 00:53:45.500
in our society, where
science continues to inform

00:53:45.500 --> 00:53:48.920
our thinking and doesn't
disenfranchise us.

00:53:48.920 --> 00:53:53.080
And this is not an easy
moment because scientists

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:56.800
know a hell of a lot, and
certainly know a great deal more

00:53:56.800 --> 00:54:01.610
than many of us, but
they don't necessarily

00:54:01.610 --> 00:54:03.460
get the right to decide for us.

00:54:03.460 --> 00:54:07.930
So it is both for the scientists
to find ways in which to

00:54:07.930 --> 00:54:11.540
engage with society about the
knowledge that they generate

00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:13.870
and are privileged
enough to have developed

00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:15.760
on our collective behalf.

00:54:15.760 --> 00:54:19.350
And it is for us to look
for fora in which we can

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:21.260
transact science.

00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:24.100
And at the end of the day, I
go back to where we started

00:54:24.100 --> 00:54:27.090
this afternoon which is, we
talk about planetary health,

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:30.240
to some of this sounds
incredibly complicated

00:54:30.240 --> 00:54:33.513
and yet a lot of it is just common sense.

00:54:34.470 --> 00:54:38.150
And what we are doing
with science is informing

00:54:38.150 --> 00:54:42.230
and empowering people
to exercise common sense

00:54:42.230 --> 00:54:45.200
with the best possible
information at our disposal.

00:54:45.200 --> 00:54:48.580
And that kind of slightly
shifts the terms of power here,

00:54:48.580 --> 00:54:53.100
because there is the temptation
for science to become almost

00:54:54.380 --> 00:54:57.940
a kind of separate universe
of decision making.

00:54:57.940 --> 00:55:01.470
I think that is not an
interest either of science

00:55:01.470 --> 00:55:02.940
nor of society.

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:07.660
And I hope that that is
perhaps a way of approaching

00:55:07.660 --> 00:55:08.493
an answer.

00:55:08.493 --> 00:55:10.723
It certainly isn't adequate
answer to the question.

00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:15.310
- Well, yeah, no, that was a great answer.

00:55:15.310 --> 00:55:16.143
Thank you.

00:55:16.143 --> 00:55:19.985
And unfortunately there's
this whole question

00:55:19.985 --> 00:55:24.985
that's developed with the
anti-vaccination et cetera

00:55:26.990 --> 00:55:28.690
that we won't have time to get into today,

00:55:28.690 --> 00:55:31.763
but it's another important
area related to science.

00:55:32.610 --> 00:55:35.700
But our time has come to an end.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:38.290
So I wanted to thank you
Administrator Steiner

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:41.350
for really fantastic conversations today.

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:45.310
I'd also like to thank
LaRon for co-moderating,

00:55:45.310 --> 00:55:49.570
Dean Kurth for our welcoming
remarks and you the audience

00:55:49.570 --> 00:55:52.510
for your engaging and
insightful questions.

00:55:52.510 --> 00:55:54.900
So thank you all for joining us today.

00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:57.530
And we look forward to
seeing you at the next event

00:55:57.530 --> 00:56:01.663
of the YIGH Global Health
Conversation series.

00:56:03.545 --> 00:56:05.629
- Thank you so much.

00:56:05.629 --> 00:56:06.587
- Thank you.

00:56:06.587 --> 00:56:08.254
- Great to have you.

